final_image_tex = SubResource("Resource_nquyw")
blurb = "This print by an unidentified artist comes from the Meiji era and depicts groups of sea bream (\"tai\").

The sea bream is associated with good fortune and abundance in Japanese culture, and eaten on special occasions as a luxury food.

The red scales are considered auspicious, and traditionally believed to ward off evil spirits. Public figures such as politicians or sumo wrestlers are sometimes photographed holding up a red sea bream after a victorious event.

Ebisu, the god of fortune and fishermen, is often portrayed holding a red sea bream.

Several Japanese idioms also reference the red sea bream as a precious object:[ul]
To fish a sea bream using a shrimp as bait (海老で鯛を釣る, ebi de tai o tsuru): To gain a great profit or achievement with minimal investment.
Even if it's rotten, it's still sea bream (腐っても鯛, kusatte mo tai): Something of high quality still retains its value even if it becomes degraded.
Rather a sardine's head than a sea bream's tail (鯛の尾より鰯の頭, tai no o yori iwashi no atama): It's better to be the leader of a small group rather than a follower of a large group.
[/ul]"
metadata/_custom_type_script = "uid://6kpsqyb2pk7a"
